Fix the engine so a clock time is read as a time, a stop written straight
against a digit does not silence the utterance, and a run of digits with a
separator in it is read rather than dropped.

  1. A separator between two runs of digits -- a space, a slash, or the colon
     of a clock time -- gets a state of its own in the transition table, so
     "5 19", "5/19" and "5:19" arrive at one handler whole. That handler
     rejected them and returned without consuming the run, so no handler was
     left and the utterance made no sound at all: every build, every position,
     so "Call 555 1234 now" was silent as well as the bare "5 19". Each run is
     now read as its own number, which is what a hyphen between them already
     does.

  2. A run can arrive with a leading character that is not a digit. A stop
     written straight against a digit -- "Read.6:23 PM", which is how an
     accessibility value often reads -- restarts the token at the stop, so
     the run is [".6"]. It was rejected, and the machine was left on the
     same token, spinning until scan()'s guard expired: no audio at all,
     and millions of repeats of one line if the tokeniser's trace is on.
     A space after the stop hid the fault, because the space is eaten and
     the token restarts on the digit. The leading non-digits are now
     skipped, so the digits after them are read.

  3. A run of digits whose comma-separated groups are not the groups of a
     grouped number -- "5,19", "3,4,5" -- used to be rejected the same way
     and was silent on every build. It is a list of numbers, and each is now
     read. A grouped number is unchanged: "1,000" is still one number, with
     the whole-part rule the build's own table describes.

     This also takes the 2006 builds' first-group rule out of the path of a
     run that is not a grouped number. That rule said the first group and
     abandoned the rest of the TEXT, so "alpha 1,000 omega zeta epsilon" came
     out the length of "alpha 1,000 omega" -- measured on 2006ENG: 12156
     samples either way. That truncation is what the original binaries do and
     is left alone for a real grouped number; it is the runs that are not
     grouped numbers that no longer take the rest of the sentence with them.

  5. A decimal whose whole part is longer than three digits -- "1234.5",
     "2026.38.0", the shape of an App Store version -- was rejected the same
     way and was silent on 19 of 20 builds, taking the rest of the utterance
     with it: "Version 2026.38.0 tail words here" made no sound at all. The
     whole part is now read as one number and the fraction spelled, which is
     what the fraction code already does for a short whole part ("123.45" is
     byte-identical before and after); anything past a second stop is given
     back, so "2026.38.0" ends "... point three eight" and ".0" is read on
     its own. A comma anywhere in the token keeps the old path.

  6. The 1998 English module's digit-name, tens and teens table addresses are
     NOT changed, and neither is the Arabic and Japanese num_silent flag.
     tests/golden.txt is the original binaries' own answers, and the pinned
     tree reproduces all 4906 of them exactly; moving any of those addresses
     changes 13 of those answers, so they are faithful to the hardware and
     "fixing" them invents behaviour the originals never had.

Apply from the root of an openbst checkout:
    git apply Patches/openbst-clock-times.patch

